[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: super_spine_router_0
diagnosis_text:
The affected component is FRR’s control-plane on super_spine_router_0, specifically the zebra daemon (and the overall frr/watchfrr service supervising it). Mechanism: vtysh “show ip route” and interface queries are served by zebra; when zebra is down, the FRR CLI cannot fetch kernel routes or interface state and returns daemon-connection errors. BGP sessions can remain Established because bgpd runs independently and peers over TCP port 179; previously installed kernel routes persist, so user traffic remains unaffected.

Localization: on super_spine_router_0, frr.service is inactive (dead), and only bgpd is running (ps shows bgpd with no zebra or watchfrr). vtysh “show ip route” returns “zebra is not running.” netstat shows bgpd’s vty (127.0.0.1:2605) and BGP (179) listeners, but no zebra vty (2601). Yet BGP neighbors to 172.16.0.1/172.16.0.3 are up (show bgp summary), and the Linux RIB still contains bgp-installed routes, indicating zebra likely started then exited and was not restarted.

Root cause: the FRR service is stopped/not supervising daemons, leaving zebra down while bgpd continues to run, which breaks FRR CLI route/interface queries.

Corrective fix: restart and re-enable the FRR service so watchfrr launches and supervises zebra and bgpd (e.g., systemctl enable --now frr). Ensure no orphan bgpd is left running (stop bgpd if needed, then start frr). Verify zebra is up (port 2601 listening) and that vtysh “show ip route” and “show interface” work again; review /var/log/frr/frr.log for any zebra crash cause. 
=== END ===